WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:12.289
Good morning ladies and gentlemen, and welcome
to this meeting of the Board of NHS England

00:00:12.289 --> 00:00:18.270
here in London. As is always our custom, we
are meeting in public. It is not a public

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:25.189
meeting, but it is an opportunity, we believe,
for our work to be under public scrutiny so

00:00:25.189 --> 00:00:30.720
that, in addition to members of the public
who we welcome here physically in person this

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:38.760
morning, we are also livestreaming across
the world the proceedings of the Board of

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:44.030
NHS England. A very warm welcome to all of
those who are joining us. We do, fundamentally,

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:51.879
believe that the public are entitled to understand
how and why decisions are taken that affect

00:00:51.879 --> 00:00:56.590
the health and welfare of the population of
England.

00:00:56.590 --> 00:01:05.449
This morning, the immediate context is the
aftermath of the general election on 7th May,

00:01:05.449 --> 00:01:11.110
in which everybody is aware that the NHS had
probably the greatest political salience of

00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:18.830
any of the many issues that the political
parties were contesting. We are conscious

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:25.220
also that throughout those discussions the
position of the Five Year Forward View, which

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:31.429
was advanced by NHS England jointly with the
other arms length bodies, was critical in

00:01:31.429 --> 00:01:36.520
trying to understand where investment needed
to go and what the priorities needed to be

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:43.670
for NHS England in the next five years. We
believe it was important and highly symbolic

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:49.080
that the Prime Minister's first public policy
speech following the general election was

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:53.860
alongside Simon Stevens in Birmingham and
talking about the next phases of the Five

00:01:53.860 --> 00:02:00.130
Year Forward View, visiting one of the vanguard
sites that is the location for one of the

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:07.640
new models of care. I know that what we feel
is our obligation as the Board of NHS England

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:12.970
is to ensure that there is clarity and stability
around policy over the coming years, so that

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:19.730
we can give effect to the vision that we enunciated
in the Forward View.

00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:25.090
As always, the meeting agenda is in two parts.
There is the public part of the meeting, and

00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:30.690
at the end of this meeting I will invite the
Board to resolve to go into private session.

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:37.120
In private session we only will take business
that, by reasons of its commercial confidentiality

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:46.310
or other reasons, could not sensibly be taken
in public. For example, this afternoon we

00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:51.190
will be spending some time looking at the
current draft of our Annual Report and Accounts,

00:02:51.190 --> 00:02:55.800
which cannot be considered in public session
because they are not to be publicised before

00:02:55.800 --> 00:03:01.350
they are laid before Parliament, which we
are reasonably confident will take place sometime

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:02.520
in July.

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:08.850
We also take the minutes of some of the committee
meetings. The one committee meeting that goes

00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:14.370
to the private session of the Board is the
Strategic, Human Resources and Remuneration

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:18.860
Committee, but I am going to make an exception
today. There is one item to which I would

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:24.420
wish to refer in public, and that relates
to the remuneration of the Chief Executive.

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:30.560
When Simon joined us in April last year he
publicly made a decision that he would waive

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:39.580
10% of his salary, and despite my fervent
discussions with him earlier this year he

00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:45.840
has decided that he would wish to renew that
waiver of the salary. I just wish to record

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:54.370
that as a matter of public record, and as
a quite selfless and highly symbolic gesture

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:59.110
on his part. Simon, thank you for that.

00:03:59.110 --> 00:04:04.430
We do not do all of our business in formal
meetings around the Board. We do have what

00:04:04.430 --> 00:04:09.640
we call development sessions, because what
we try to do is to ensure that the Board works

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:17.750
as non executives and executives in a tight
partnership as we work up early stages of

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:23.320
future business. Yesterday we had a development
session in which we looked at how we might

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:30.280
bring further papers to the Board of the coming
year relating to taking stock post election

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:34.620
and the next steps that we needed to take
on policy development.

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:39.040
I should also stress that all members of the
Board are constantly engaged in the work of

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:44.889
committees and in events outside the boardroom
itself.

00:04:44.889 --> 00:04:51.000
Finally, just to repeat something that I said
in preliminary comments at the last meeting.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:57.990
We do circulate all Board papers electronically.
We are so switched on digitally as a Board,

00:04:57.990 --> 00:05:04.370
and this is both efficient and sustainable,
but if you see members of the Board referring

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:08.100
to their iPads during the course of the meeting
it is because the papers are on here. We can

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:14.340
not only receive the papers, but we can annotate
them and write notes on them as we go. That

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:29.180
is just to assure you that Angry Birds and
Candy Crush are not being played by members

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:37.090
of the Board, whose whole attention is focused
on the business of the Board during this session.

00:05:37.090 --> 00:05:55.070
That brings me to the formal opening of the
meeting. I

00:05:55.070 --> 00:06:01.919
invite any declarations of interest in matters
on the Agenda that are not otherwise previously

00:06:01.919 --> 00:06:10.280
declared in registration. There are none.

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:14.090
Lord Victor Adebowale is not with us this
morning, but I have a confession to make,

00:06:14.090 --> 00:06:18.020
which is that we owe him apologies rather
than the other way around, because when we

00:06:18.020 --> 00:06:21.639
scheduled this meeting we were aware that
he was not able to make it. We simply had

00:06:21.639 --> 00:06:32.350
to press ahead because of the lack of availability
of suitable times.

00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:37.270
We have starred items which relate to reports
of the committees. I have not had any indication

00:06:37.270 --> 00:06:43.370
from members to have those items un starred,
so we shall take them as business which is

00:06:43.370 --> 00:06:47.280
simply to be noted, unless anybody during
the course of the meeting expresses a wish

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:48.430
to the contrary.

00:06:48.430 --> 00:06:54.910
Let me move to the next phase of the business,
which is to consider the minutes of the Board

00:06:54.910 --> 00:07:01.560
meeting from 26th March. Those minutes have
been circulated. I have had no comments on

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:05.380
them, so I ask you to approve them as an accurate
record of the last meeting.

00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:12.729
Moira Gibb: There is an inaccuracy, in that
I am recorded as Moira Grant and not Moira

00:07:12.729 --> 00:07:13.500
Gibb.

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:23.290
Malcom Grant: May I say how immensely flattered
I am by that comparison and how shocked you

00:07:23.290 --> 00:07:30.820
must be. If we could record that Moira Gibb
is indeed Moira Gibb, I would be grateful.

00:07:30.820 --> 00:07:34.710
Apologies that that was not picked up, Moira.

00:07:34.710 --> 00:07:39.770
Can I also invite any matters arising from
the minutes? I do not identify any matters

00:07:39.770 --> 00:07:46.520
that are not otherwise on the Agenda. In that
case, please may we move on to the Chief Executive's

00:07:46.520 --> 00:07:47.509
report?

00:07:47.509 --> 00:07:54.759
Simon Stevens: I would just draw out three
items from the written report. The first is

00:07:54.759 --> 00:08:03.000
it is worth spending a moment looking back
at 2014/15 in terms of the operations. Secondly,

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:11.220
a few headlines from the work underway to
conclude the 2015/16 commissioning round;

00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:16.720
we will talk more about that later on in the
meeting. Thirdly, since we last met we have

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:22.660
published our business plan for this coming
year, setting out our ten overarching objectives,

00:08:22.660 --> 00:08:26.680
and it would be worth briefly reflecting on
those.

00:08:28.900 --> 00:08:35.419
In terms of 2014/15, we will be publishing
our Annual Report by the end of July, but

00:08:35.419 --> 00:08:44.050
as the Board has seen through the course of
the year, 2014/15 was a stretching year for

00:08:44.050 --> 00:08:49.110
NHS England, our second year of operation.
Nevertheless, there is a lot that we can feel

00:08:49.110 --> 00:08:56.240
good about. I would group the work of the
organisation under three broad headings: ensuring

00:08:56.240 --> 00:09:02.389
performance in 2014/15; setting strategic
direction for the NHS over the coming five

00:09:02.389 --> 00:09:10.660
years; and improving our own capability and
the changeability of the system as a whole.

00:09:10.660 --> 00:09:16.399
In terms of the 2014/15 outturn, as everybody
knows, there were real service pressures right

00:09:16.399 --> 00:09:22.089
across the National Health Service, strong
demand pressures and pressures on providers.

00:09:22.089 --> 00:09:30.110
Despite that fact, fundamentally, performance
levels remained good. Obviously we worked

00:09:30.110 --> 00:09:37.240
closely with the Department of Health and
Treasury to secure additional funding during

00:09:37.240 --> 00:09:43.459
the course of the year, to make fairer allocations
as a Board to CCGs, halving the number of

00:09:43.459 --> 00:09:51.670
CCGs more than 5% under their target share,
from 34 to 17. We met our overriding financial

00:09:51.670 --> 00:09:58.279
duties, as Paul Baumann will report in a moment.
We took important steps to devolve power and

00:09:58.279 --> 00:10:05.429
promote autonomy across the National Health
Service, with 151 CCGs having more influence

00:10:05.429 --> 00:10:11.920
and control over the way in which primary
care services were organised, the ground breaking

00:10:11.920 --> 00:10:19.199
partnership agreement with Manchester, and
the launch of Integrated Personal Commissioning

00:10:19.199 --> 00:10:25.399
to put more power into the hands of patients
and their families.

00:10:25.399 --> 00:10:30.389
Alongside that, the first steps on strengthening
primary care, recognising this is going to

00:10:30.389 --> 00:10:36.769
be one of the key things that collectively
we have to put our energy into. Since our

00:10:36.769 --> 00:10:41.970
last Board meeting, we have made the first
set of allocations for GPs across the country

00:10:41.970 --> 00:10:47.879
from the £250 million premises and infrastructure
investment fund. You will remember we have

00:10:47.879 --> 00:10:52.809
a further £0.75 billion that we are going
to be able to invest over the next three years

00:10:52.809 --> 00:10:59.009
to help strengthen general practice on top
of the other work that we are doing as part

00:10:59.009 --> 00:11:04.879
of the 10 point plan with the Royal College
of GPs and the General Practitioners Committee.

00:11:04.879 --> 00:11:11.679
We stabilised specialised commissioning during
the course of 2014/15 under the leadership

00:11:11.679 --> 00:11:17.399
of the new specialised commissioning task
force, and managed the Cancer Drugs Fund and

00:11:17.399 --> 00:11:22.019
various other high-profile commissioning decisions,
recognising that we still have further work

00:11:22.019 --> 00:11:26.199
to do during the course of 2015/16.

00:11:26.199 --> 00:11:32.199
We began the move towards mental health parity
of esteem using ring fenced money for investment

00:11:32.199 --> 00:11:38.189
in child and adolescent mental health services
and eating disorders. We set clear direction

00:11:38.189 --> 00:11:48.829
for the NHS for 2015/16, expecting CCGs to
increase their investment in real terms in

00:11:48.829 --> 00:11:55.790
mental health services in each area, consistent
with the overall funding growth that they

00:11:55.790 --> 00:12:00.509
themselves were receiving. When we talk later
about the assurance process we are using for

00:12:00.509 --> 00:12:07.629
CCG investment decisions for 2015/16, we can
update on where that has reached.

00:12:07.629 --> 00:12:13.189
There was significant progress on the technology
and information agenda, patient and public

00:12:13.189 --> 00:12:16.889
involvement with the NHS Citizen programme.

00:12:16.889 --> 00:12:21.679
In the round, therefore, there were a lot
of good things in the here and now during

00:12:21.679 --> 00:12:23.739
the course of 2014/15.

00:12:23.739 --> 00:12:28.600
Over and above that, the second big thing
we did, which really bears no repeating, is

00:12:28.600 --> 00:12:31.809
with partners we established the strategic
direction for the National Health Service

00:12:31.809 --> 00:12:39.619
for the next five years. As you said, Malcolm,
the broad consensus on display from all political

00:12:39.619 --> 00:12:45.509
parties during the election campaign, and
the clear message delivered by the Queen in

00:12:45.509 --> 00:12:49.759
the Queen's Speech yesterday that it is game
on as far as the Five Year Forward View implementation

00:12:49.759 --> 00:12:56.360
is concerned, is testament to the work of
a broad base of stakeholders right across

00:12:56.360 --> 00:13:01.730
the health and social care system, and with
voluntary sector and community partners as

00:13:01.730 --> 00:13:02.299
well.

00:13:02.299 --> 00:13:08.149
In terms of capability building, over the
course of two years we have cut the running

00:13:08.149 --> 00:13:14.629
costs of the commissioning system in England
by over 30%. At the same time, we have sought

00:13:14.629 --> 00:13:22.189
to better align the matching of talent and
effort to core priorities, including, under

00:13:22.189 --> 00:13:29.189
your Chairmanship, reshaping the committee
structures and the workings of this Board.

00:13:29.189 --> 00:13:35.610
We have set more emphasis on the need for
a more diverse workforce across the National

00:13:35.610 --> 00:13:39.360
Health Service with the implementation of
the Workforce Race Equality Standard under

00:13:39.360 --> 00:13:46.449
the NHS Equality and Diversity Committee.
We know there is much further work required

00:13:46.449 --> 00:13:54.660
there. Ed's review on the future of leadership
and improvement, which will be published shortly,

00:13:54.660 --> 00:14:01.329
is going to set a direction of travel for
improvement that has perhaps been too diffuse

00:14:01.329 --> 00:14:07.519
in terms of the architecture of institutional
structures over the last several years, and

00:14:07.519 --> 00:14:12.410
we know we need to correct that with Monitor,
TDA and others.

00:14:12.410 --> 00:14:18.389
All of which is just to say that it is easy
in the heat of battle to lose track of the

00:14:18.389 --> 00:14:21.939
fact that we have come an enormous distance
over the course of the last year. I want to

00:14:21.939 --> 00:14:29.410
put on record my thanks to the executive directors
and all of the employees of NHS England for

00:14:29.410 --> 00:14:36.699
those achievements during what inevitably
has been a difficult year given the pressures,

00:14:36.699 --> 00:14:41.949
the running cost reductions, and in some cases
the redundancies that we have had to embark

00:14:41.949 --> 00:14:42.199
on.

00:14:42.199 --> 00:14:48.929
The second thing I will touch on is the state
of play with the annual commissioning round.

00:14:48.929 --> 00:14:55.709
As I say in my report, and as we will hear
later from Paul and Barbara, with our CCG

00:14:55.709 --> 00:15:07.170
partners we are having to strike a pragmatic
balance between the desire to invest in services

00:15:07.170 --> 00:15:15.249
that offset the continuing increases in emergency
demand and elective activity on the one hand,

00:15:15.249 --> 00:15:24.269
and on the other, providing realistic, upfront
commitments to providers as to the volume

00:15:24.269 --> 00:15:28.189
of patients they are likely to need to treat
so that they can make good capacity planning

00:15:28.189 --> 00:15:33.379
decisions for the year. We think we are striking
that balance with an assumption of activity

00:15:33.379 --> 00:15:39.769
growth in the 2-3% range, which will obviously
vary in different parts of the country. Those

00:15:39.769 --> 00:15:44.519
activity growth numbers are probably not yet
fully reflected in providers' own plans and

00:15:44.519 --> 00:15:50.429
finances, so we expect to see some further
improvement in their position as that happens.

00:15:50.429 --> 00:15:54.799
All of this is obviously playing out against
the backdrop of the very difficult situation

00:15:54.799 --> 00:16:01.040
in provider finances across the National Health
Service. Monitor's board meeting today and

00:16:01.040 --> 00:16:08.879
TDA's last week show substantial overspending
by NHS providers during the course of 2014/15,

00:16:08.879 --> 00:16:15.829
and it is our collective responsibility - including
Monitor, TDA, the Department of Health, NHS

00:16:15.829 --> 00:16:22.079
England - to help providers now manage the
source of those cost pressures.

00:16:22.079 --> 00:16:31.449
The £822 million net overspend by providers
is more than explained by, in fact, one single

00:16:31.449 --> 00:16:35.519
factor. There are other things going on as
well under the delivery of planned efficiencies,

00:16:35.519 --> 00:16:43.269
but the single most important factor is that,
as the public papers for Monitor and TDA show,

00:16:43.269 --> 00:16:51.369
NHS providers overspent on temporary staffing
by £1.4 billion in 2014/15. For example,

00:16:51.369 --> 00:17:03.429
the FT sector was budgeted to spend £770
million on temporary staffing and spent £1.77

00:17:03.429 --> 00:17:10.829
billion on temporary staffing. Therefore,
we have a clear agreement across the sector

00:17:10.829 --> 00:17:17.270
for concerted collective action on temporary
staffing to convert temporary staffing into

00:17:17.270 --> 00:17:21.940
permanent jobs to ensure that NHS hospitals
are able to offer the sort of flexible employment

00:17:21.940 --> 00:17:27.620
opportunities that will be attractive to staff,
combined with continuing work to increase

00:17:27.620 --> 00:17:35.710
the output from nurse training and to ensure
retention. This workforce piece is going to

00:17:35.710 --> 00:17:42.370
be absolutely essential to helping providers
manage their costs this year and next, and

00:17:42.370 --> 00:17:48.260
we expect to see a programme of action launched
shortly to do just that.

00:17:48.260 --> 00:17:57.300
Alongside the 2014/15 look back and the 2015/16
commissioning round, obviously we have, as

00:17:57.300 --> 00:18:01.540
I said, published our business plan. You will
see the last page of my report identifies

00:18:01.540 --> 00:18:08.320
the ten key goals for the year that the Board
approved at our last meeting, and we are now

00:18:08.320 --> 00:18:15.450
mobilising around this, particularly on cancer
and mental health through the two task forces

00:18:15.450 --> 00:18:20.510
that have been established. We expect the
Cancer Taskforce to be in a position to publish

00:18:20.510 --> 00:18:27.280
its work this side of the summer break, and
the Mental Health Taskforce probably in the

00:18:27.280 --> 00:18:32.640
autumn. Jane is going to describe to you the
next steps of the work on learning disabilities,

00:18:32.640 --> 00:18:40.570
and we are now ramping up for the diabetes
prevention and obesity work, which again has

00:18:40.570 --> 00:18:46.930
received support from across government for
some of the broader determinants that are

00:18:46.930 --> 00:18:51.740
obviously not within our direct control.

00:18:51.740 --> 00:18:57.350
On urgent emergency care, primary care, elective
care, specialised care, the care redesign

00:18:57.350 --> 00:19:02.650
and the four priorities there, we will bring
to you specific proposals over the course

00:19:02.650 --> 00:19:09.440
of the next three Board meetings and we will
have a chance to talk about some of the enablers

00:19:09.440 --> 00:19:10.920
later on in this session.

00:19:10.920 --> 00:19:14.050
I will stop there and take any questions that
arise.

00:19:14.050 --> 00:19:17.750
Malcolm Grant: We will be coming back, as
you said, to the commissioning round discussion,

00:19:17.750 --> 00:19:22.130
but if there are questions on other parts
of Simon's report please may we take them

00:19:22.130 --> 00:19:22.990
now.

00:19:22.990 --> 00:19:31.550
A chairman should never lose the opportunity
that silence gives to press on with business,

00:19:31.550 --> 00:19:35.440
so may I ask us please to note the Chief Executive's
report.

